    keywords: 'record set up first payment first deal new escrow buying agent limits renew agent 1005 10000 one year earnings own wallet both records paid on both your records count my deals protected deals web3 wallet signature network fee',
    summary: 'New deals open on Karwan\'s new escrow once it is switched on for a network; deals already open finish where they started. A person\'s Karwan record holds their deals and checks, and links their buying and selling agents to them. Nothing ever waits for it: paying, agreeing to terms, a seller\'s own offer and their selling agent\'s offers all go ahead with or without a record. Where Karwan holds the person\'s wallets (email, passkey and Telegram accounts), Karwan sets the record up itself the moment it is first needed, with no step from them, on testnet; on mainnet they confirm it is them with a passkey or PIN. Someone with their own wallet never takes a step for it either: the steps still missing ride along with the next transaction they sign on Arc in Karwan (adding money to their agents, putting up a deal guarantee, claiming yield, accepting a delivery and more), in the same wallet prompt, only when their USDC covers the small extra network fee, and never when they send their whole balance. Their own transaction always comes first and never fails because of the record. Once it is set up, the result says: Your account is also set up for protected deals. A transaction signed on another chain, or from a smart-contract wallet, goes alone and the next one carries the record. A deal paid in before a side had a record runs normally but does not count on that side\'s record. If that happens to someone with their own wallet, the settled deal\'s receipt shows one quiet line, Count your next deals on your record, with a button that signs the missing steps alone; it shows at most once a month. set_up_record also sets it up on request. The buying agent can pay up to 1,005 USDC at a time and 10,000 USDC in total for one year; when it reaches its year or its total, it steps aside so a payment still goes through, and it is linked again with the next setup. After each payment the seller\'s earnings move from their selling agent to their own wallet; a move still waiting shows in their balance and is tried again. On mainnet, deals can be up to 1,000 USDC while Karwan is in beta; testnet allows larger deals. A deal over the limit for that network is refused before any money moves, with the largest amount allowed. Once a deal settles, the deal page says whether it is on both records, and each side sees whether it is on theirs.',
    steps: ['Nothing to do first: pay, agree or offer as usual.', 'With your own wallet, sign your next Arc transaction in Karwan as usual; it carries the record when your balance allows, and says so after.', 'If a settled deal did not count, tap Count them on its receipt, or ask the assistant to set up your record.'],
