    summary: 'Each person has one agent, and what it may do reads as one thing (show_my_mandate): what it sells and from what lowest and usual price, the most it spends on a new request, whether it makes offers on its own and asks before any deal, asks first about every fitting request, or agrees to a buyer’s price inside their limits, its weekly limit for paid checks (0 to 5 USDC), the markets it watches, up to five goals in the person’s words (buy, sell or watch, matched on words, no model), when the daily brief comes (8:00 Lagos time unless changed) and quiet hours (21:00 to 7:00 unless changed or turned off). One part changes at a time, and only through a card the person confirms (propose_change_mandate); the card shows the line before and after and what the change does next, for example how many requests a lower price re-opens. Once confirmed the agent acts at once: a lower lowest price looks again at the requests it passed on for price this week, leaving ask first closes those questions and judges the requests again, and the weekly limit applies to the next paid check. A request’s budget is fixed on chain, so a new buying limit applies to new requests; the card says which open requests ask for more. Once a day at the brief hour, the agent sends a short brief built from the person’s own data with no model: new offers on their requests, questions waiting on them and the first deadline, deals waiting on them, up to three things it sees about how they trade (three a day at most, counting live notes), and a line per goal. No brief when there is nothing to say. It goes to Telegram when linked, else the bell and the assistant on Karwan, and by email only when they have neither. In quiet hours the agent sends nothing but a question’s own reminder; a live note waits for the brief. Everything it told them is kept 30 days (what_did_my_agent_do). Changing limits moves no money; paying still needs the person.',
