    summary: 'A free beta on Karwan’s Telegram bot, one bot per network, switched on per network. /start asks whether the person has a Karwan account or is new. Either way they sign in with an email and a 6-digit code, and the email is the account: the same one the website signs in. Someone who signs in to Karwan with a wallet signs in on the website and taps Connect Telegram, which opens their own chat and links it. A new person accepts the chat terms, picks a Karwan tag (their Telegram username is offered when it fits the rules), and answers the same About you questions as the website, in the same order, with buttons for the choices; how clients pay and how much the agent does alone start from the defaults the website uses. Here is your Karwan follows as a card, and once they confirm it the chat offers to post their first offer or request. Their Telegram username is saved on the account, and people can find them by it only if they allow it. Anyone can ask the agent to check a person by tag or Telegram name and gets the public record with one line of advice. The last setup question in the chat is which country they are in, since a chat has no Profile page to add it later; only the country is shown, and city and area are used to match them with people near them. When it finds buyers in a new country it asks whether they deliver there before offering the work. Anyone can ask their agent to message someone by Karwan tag before any deal (message_person); the person gets it in their Karwan chat with an alert on Telegram and by email, sees the sender’s tag, country and tier, and answers with their agent’s help (read_my_messages). On the website every conversation is listed in Messages (/messages, also from the alerts bell, with how many have a new message; opening a conversation, or reading it through the agent, clears its alert without a reply), and each conversation (/messages/<tag>) shows the other person’s photo, name, tag, country and tier, with Start a protected deal, which opens a direct deal with them. Your own messages show in ink and theirs on a light card; the round Send button is the one green control, and an unread alert keeps a small green dot. Someone whose first messages to five people in a day all go unanswered is paused from writing to anyone new for a day, and three messages in a row with no answer wait for a reply. Only someone with a Karwan tag can message, and a message with a link Karwan flags is not sent. A message with a phone number, an email or a link before any deal between the two carries the warning to keep payment in Karwan escrow, and so does any message, here or in a deal chat, that asks to be paid outside Karwan (for example send it to my account, an OPay or bank account number, or no need for escrow): it still goes through, both sides see the warning and the Karwan team is told. A market listing, a request a seller is looking at, and a public record page have Message @tag, so a buyer can ask before agreeing; the first message names the ad it is about (message_person with about), and Start a protected deal in the conversation opens the deal form. Messages are kept 14 days. When they agree, either agent starts the protected deal; money never moves from a message. Links they send are added to their record as self-added, which never raises the score. Alerts come through the same bot and can be muted by asking. People get verified, find verified sellers or customers and agree deals in chat. Agreements, delivery and disputes are confirmed with chat buttons; every step that moves money opens a one-time Karwan link and is confirmed on that page. Karwan holds the wallets of chat accounts; a web account that linked Telegram talks to the agent as itself. A past client confirms a job from a link the person forwards, and it counts once that client is person verified. The WhatsApp agent is paused. Escrow on Arc mainnet is a beta, up to 1,000 USDC per deal and not audited; deals of 100 USDC and above are not open in the beta.',
